submcu: fast-forward the idle loop between events

Measured with real mk2 ROMs over a five-minute track, the sub-MCU executed 766
million instructions and slept for 0.02% of them; 98.2% were one twenty-
instruction loop -- the same twenty addresses, each executed 37,639,226 times,
so one deterministic path -- that reads and writes nothing but zero-page RAM
bytes 00..03 and leaves them as it found them. It is the firmware's idle loop,
spinning at 127,000 passes a second until a timer or UART interrupt, and it
was around a sixth of everything the emulator does.

A loop that touches only zero-page RAM and reproduces its own state is
invisible to the main MCU and to the outside world, so between events the
machine's state at any point of any later pass is exactly the recorded state
at that position. This patch detects that condition -- it never assumes it:
a candidate head is the target of a backward transfer, one pass is recorded
from there, and it counts as stationary only if it was pure (no access outside
zero-page RAM, marked by SM_Read/SM_Write), no interrupt was dispatched, the
sub-MCU never slept, and registers and the whole zero page are identical to
the previous visit. From then on SM_Update advances the clock across whole
passes to the earliest of the next timer fire (closed form from the counter,
prescaler and reload), the delivery of a pending UART byte, and a cap, never
past a pending interrupt request, and installs the recorded registers for the
landing position.

That may leave the sub-MCU's clock ahead of the main MCU's. Every external
access -- SM_SysRead, SM_SysWrite, MCU_PostUART -- first calls SM_FF_Sync,
which rewinds to the position the true machine would be at, restoring the
registers for it and recomputing the timer from its state at the jump origin,
valid because no fire lay inside the window. The events that were bounded
against are then handled by the unchanged per-instruction code.

Equivalence: the core's real submcu.cpp compiled twice, upstream renamed, both
driven by the same simulated main MCU -- random shared-RAM reads and writes,
UART bytes posted at random cycles, the real timer -- over the real sub-MCU
firmware and over synthetic loops written for the test, comparing every
observable and the full state at every synchronisation point, with mutants.
See patches/tests/mcu/sm_fastforward_equivalence.cpp. Real-ROM renders of
01.mid and 17.mid are byte-identical to the pre-patch core on both romsets.

